  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <title>{title} | Biashivo Business Insights</title>
    <style>
      :root {
        font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
        color: #24364b;
        background: #f7fafc;
      }

      * { box-sizing: border-box; }

      body { margin: 0; }

      a { color: #008e74; }

      .container {
        width: min(1120px, 92%);
        margin: auto;
      }

      header {
        background: #0c2033;
        padding: 24px 0;
      }

      .header-inner {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: space-between;
        gap: 20px;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
      }

      .brand {
        color: white;
        text-decoration: none;
        font-size: 23px;
        font-weight: 800;
        letter-spacing: 1px;
      }

      .brand span { color: #00c9a7; }

      .back-link {
        color: #d3e1e9;
        text-decoration: none;
        font-size: 14px;
      }

      .article {
        width: min(800px, 90%);
        margin: 65px auto 85px;
        background: white;
        padding: clamp(24px, 5vw, 65px);
        border: 1px solid #e2e9ef;
        border-radius: 16px;
      }

      .category {
        color: #008e74;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        font-weight: bold;
        font-size: 13px;
        letter-spacing: 1.5px;
      }

      h1 {
        color: #0c2033;
        font-size: clamp(32px, 5vw, 48px);
        line-height: 1.18;
        margin: 18px 0 24px;
      }

      .intro {
        font-size: 19px;
        line-height: 1.85;
        color: #43566a;
        margin-bottom: 38px;
      }

      h2 {
        color: #0c2033;
        font-size: 27px;
        margin: 42px 0 15px;
        line-height: 1.35;
      }

      p, li {
        font-size: 17px;
        line-height: 1.9;
      }

      ul { padding-left: 24px; }

      li { margin-bottom: 10px; }

      .example {
        background: #eaf4f3;
        border-left: 4px solid #00a989;
        padding: 18px 24px;
        border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
        margin: 28px 0;
      }

      .example p { margin: 0; }

      .closing {
        margin-top: 48px;
        padding-top: 24px;
        border-top: 1px solid #e2e9ef;
      }

      .button {
        display: inline-block;
        margin-top: 20px;
        background: #00a989;
        color: white;
        padding: 14px 22px;
        border-radius: 8px;
        text-decoration: none;
        font-weight: bold;
      }

      footer {
        background: #071726;
        color: #cbd5df;
        text-align: center;
        padding: 32px 16px;
        font-size: 14px;
      }

      @media (max-width: 600px) {
        .article { margin: 25px auto 50px; }
        h2 { font-size: 23px; }
      }
    </style>
  </head>

  <body>
    <header>
      <div class="container header-inner">
        <a class="brand" href="/">BIASHI<span>VO</span></a>
        <a class="back-link" href="/#articles">← Back to Business Insights</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main>
      <article class="article">
        <div class="category">Cash Flow &amp; Business Management</div>

        <h1>
          The Hidden Cost of Running an SME in Kenya
          (And Why It Doesn't Have to Be This Way)
        </h1>

        <p class="intro">
          Ask a cake maker, caterer, shopkeeper, or small business owner
          in Kenya what makes running a business difficult, and you'll
          often hear about challenges that happen after a sale:
          tracking payments, managing expenses, and knowing how much
          money the business has actually earned.
        </p>

        <h2>1. "Who Owes Me What?"</h2>

        <p>
          For many small businesses, customer orders and payments are
          recorded in notebooks, WhatsApp conversations, spreadsheets,
          or simply remembered.
        </p>

        <p>
          Imagine a cake maker who receives a deposit for a wedding cake.
          The customer promises to pay the balance before collection.
          Meanwhile, several other customers are placing orders and
          paying different amounts.
        </p>

        <p>
          Without a clear record, it becomes difficult to remember
          who has paid in full, who has made a deposit, and who
          still owes money.
        </p>

        <div class="example">
          <p>
            <strong>Example:</strong> A customer orders a cake for
            KSh 8,000 and pays a deposit of KSh 3,000.
            The outstanding balance is KSh 5,000.
            If that balance isn't recorded properly, the business
            owner may forget to follow up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>2. Chasing Payments Feels Uncomfortable</h2>

        <p>
          Following up on unpaid balances can be one of the most
          uncomfortable parts of running a small business.
          Business owners want to maintain good relationships
          with customers, so they sometimes delay sending reminders.
        </p>

        <p>
          But delayed follow-ups can affect the money available
          to buy supplies, pay workers, and fulfill new orders.
        </p>

        <p>
          A simple payment tracking process helps business owners
          know exactly which balances are outstanding and when
          to follow up professionally.
        </p>

        <h2>3. Sales Are Not the Same as Profit</h2>

        <p>
          Making KSh 50,000 in sales does not mean a business
          has earned KSh 50,000 in profit.
        </p>

        <p>
          The business must first account for the costs of producing
          or delivering its products and services, along with
          other operating expenses.
        </p>

        <div class="example">
          <p>
            <strong>Simple example:</strong><br />
            Sales: KSh 50,000<br />
            Ingredients and supplies: KSh 18,000<br />
            Delivery and transport: KSh 4,000<br />
            Other operating expenses: KSh 8,000<br />
            <strong>Estimated profit: KSh 20,000</strong>
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          When business owners track both income and expenses,
          they can make better decisions about pricing, spending,
          and future growth.
        </p>

        <h2>4. Poor Cash Flow Creates Unexpected Problems</h2>

        <p>
          A business can appear profitable on paper while still
          struggling to pay its immediate bills.
        </p>

        <p>
          This can happen when customers owe money, stock purchases
          are due, or business funds are mixed with personal spending.
        </p>

        <p>
          Knowing when money is expected to come in and when
          expenses must be paid helps business owners plan ahead.
        </p>

        <h2>5. Scattered Records Waste Valuable Time</h2>

        <p>
          When customer details, orders, payments, and expenses
          are stored in different places, even simple questions
          become difficult to answer.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>How much did the business sell this week?</li>
          <li>Which customers still owe money?</li>
          <li>How much was spent on supplies?</li>
          <li>Which products or services are most profitable?</li>
          <li>How much money is available for the next order?</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          A consistent record-keeping system can reduce the time
          spent searching through messages and notebooks.
        </p>

        <h2>6. Small Changes Can Make a Big Difference</h2>

        <p>
          Improving business management doesn't always require
          expensive software or complicated accounting knowledge.
        </p>

        <p>
          Start with a few simple habits:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>Record every sale when it happens.</li>
          <li>Track deposits and outstanding customer balances.</li>
          <li>Record business expenses consistently.</li>
          <li>Review sales, expenses, and profit every week.</li>
          <li>Keep business and personal money separate where possible.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Building a Better Way for African SMEs</h2>

        <p>
          At Biashivo, we believe small business owners deserve
          simple tools that help them understand their numbers
          without adding unnecessary complexity.
        </p>

        <p>
          That's why we're building Biashivo: a business management
          platform designed to bring sales, expenses, customers,
          orders, and profit information together in one place.
        </p>

        <p>
          We're starting in Kenya, with a vision to support
          entrepreneurs across Africa.
        </p>

        <div class="closing">
          <h2>Know your numbers. Grow your business.</h2>
          <p>
            The first step toward a stronger business is
            understanding where your money comes from,
            where it goes, and what remains.
          </p>

